show's original run  before it was rebooted in 2018 and the  big change was due to the conor's  winning the lottery  we won the lottery  this is the winning lottery ticket i've  got it right here remember you told me  to watch it on tv why are you talking we  won the lottery from ordinary working  class struggles the show's focus shifted  to pop culture parody and a  self-conscious approach additionally  roseanne's husband dan was sidelined for  most of the season explained away by  subplots involving him taking care of  his mother and cheating on his wife the  finale didn't offer much satisfaction it  completely invalidated the entire season  by revealing that it was all happening  in roseanne's imagination dan had  actually died from a heart attack after  season 8. the connors didn't really win  the lottery and roseanne was actually  writing her memoir as she spiraled  further down into sadness it was the  show's lowest rated season and it left a  bad taste in fans mouths that wasn't  fully washed away until the revival  undid some of the most hated decisions  lasting 9 seasons from 1989 to 1998  seinfeld was one of the most successful  and beloved american sitcoms of the  decade featuring comedian jerry seinfeld  delivering his signature brand of  observational humor the so-called show  about nothing featured him and his three  friends george elaine and kramer in  mundane situations that brought out the  best or worst in them in new york city  one infamous example was the 1997  episode the puerto rican day which  invited a ton of backlash to the show's  doorstep the premise was simple enough  the main characters found themselves  unable to move through traffic because  of the city's puerto rican day parade  this triggered a series of events that  led to kramer accidentally burning the  puerto rican flag with a sparkler the  outrage in response to that moment was  swift and tremendous and it offended  many new yorkers particularly the  hispanic population this was enough for  colombia tristar to knock it out of  circulation in new york for almost 5  years  among all the reasons why fans would  feel angry and betrayed by their  favorite tv show you would think that  the lead character getting a haircut  would be pretty low on the list but  that's not how things turned out for  carrie russell star of the wb's college  set drama felicity in one episode that  aired in 1999 she cut off her famous  curly hair which seemingly summoned to  the apocalypse in a 2017 interview with  w magazine russell noted that the  haircut was intended to be part of the  character's post-breakup actions in the  series and while the actress herself  enjoyed her new hairstyle she quickly  realized that the fans didn't share that  opinion as she put it i did not expect  all the hysteria strangers did come up  to me on the street and say things like  you were so pretty before you cut your  hair the show's ratings reportedly  plummeted to the point that wb  entertainment president suzanne daniels  believed that the haircut turned off  many of the show's male fans as she put  it you deluded that icon i think it  turned some of the audience away in  particular men and some women the move  generated so much negative buzz that  it's even been immortalized in  contemporary lexicon as pulling a  felicity  move it's like a whole new you  if you take a look at rotten tomatoes  scoreboard for each year of buffy the  vampire slayer you'll notice that season  6 sits at the bottom with 63 percent  this isn't just a case of critics being  overly critical as recounted by vanity  fair the sixth season of the iconic  supernatural 90s show was also the most  hated by its fans for numerous reasons  for an important bit of perspective  season five ended with sarah michelle  geller's title character heroically  sacrificing herself to save the world  season 6 then dealt with her friends  attempting to bring her back and  eventually succeeding however the series  took an overall depressing tone mired  with weird plot twists gratuitous sex  and a protagonist who had trouble  dealing with her resurrection to make  matters worse the cast reportedly had  difficulty communicating their issues  with the story to show runner joss  whedon who was busy balancing his  workload with two other shows in  addition to buffy fans even coined a  pair of derogatory nicknames for the  less than stellar season season sex and  season sucks  debuting in 1994 the abc sitcom ellen  featured the day-to-day life of a  30-something bookstore owner as she  balanced the little struggles and  triumphs of life in los angeles it  catapulted its star future talk show  host ellen degeneres to super stardom  this was for two main reasons for one it  was the vehicle for many viewers to get  acquainted with her quirky sense of  humor and furthermore the show made  history by having its main character  reveal her true sexual orientation at  around the same time the actor playing  her did so in real life unsurprisingly  this move prompted both congratulations  and controversy the plan to have the  on-screen ellen come out on the show had  been in the works since 1996 and so in  1997's two-part the puppy episode she  admits the truth to her love interest  susan played by laura dern via an  unintentional and humorous public  declaration  susan  i'm  [Applause]  gay puppy episode garnered 42 million  viewers becoming the highest rated  outing of the series degeneres's bold  move earned the episode in the show many  accolades awards and recognition  unfortunately it also drew the ire of  multiple advertisers and religious  groups as well as angry fans who  reportedly sent hate mail and even  threatened to bomb the production both  degeneres and dern suffered career  setbacks after the episode aired  although they've since managed to  recover and continue to work on popular  projects  sometimes a tv show has a premise so  fundamentally problematic you wonder how  it ever made it onto the air in the  first place consider the case of heil  honey i'm home a british sitcom that  followed a fictionalized adolf hitler  and ava braun as a quirky couple in a  corny 1950s inspired setting their  next-door neighbors were a jewish couple  called the goldensteins only one episode  aired in 1990 before a combination of  behind the scenes disagreements  production woes and protests from groups  offended by the idea of trivializing  hitler took it off the air completely in  a 2017 interview with entertainment  weekly series creator jeff atkinson  explained the thought process behind the  show the idea was that he wanted to beat  the bully by laughing at him as he puts  it if you have a monster like that and  everyone says you can't make fun of him  then we've made him even more of a  monster that's what fascists want to  keep people in fear of them when surely  we should be debunking and destroying  them  in december 1997 hundreds of children in  japan tuned in to get their regular dose  of pokemon but by the end of this  particular episode they found themselves  experiencing seizures and in the days  that followed many more were rushed to  hospitals the root of this medical  emergency was a six second sequence of  bright flashing lights the episode  entitled electric soldier porygon  generated so much controversy that the  show aired no new episodes for four  months the series was ultimately saved  from the jaws of cancellation but  nintendo ended up banning the episode in  all countries furthermore the pokemon  character porygon and its evolutions  have yet to appear or display their  powers on the show since the incident  and that's not the end of the story a  2001 study looked into the incident and  found that the cases of children  suffering seizures after the episode  aired may not have been due to epilepsy  instead they were likely the result of  mass hysteria a phenomenon in which  people experience severe physical  symptoms due to stress or a perceived  threat  sometimes even the most well-intentioned  attempts at social awareness can  backfire especially when you attempt  them in a children's cartoon one major  example is the 1991 tiny toon adventures  episode elephant issues whose satirical  segment about the dangers of drunk  driving caused it to be banned from  reruns on fox kids the episode's third  short entitled one beer features buster  bunny plucky duck and hampton j pig  engaging in some car stealing  shenanigans after drinking a single  bottle of beer as soon as they got  intoxicated they managed to carjack a  police vehicle then they end up driving  off a cliff seemingly killing themselves  and ascending into heaven as angels but  then the true punchline arrives seconds  later when the camera pants to reveal  the three characters alive and unscathed  in a studio the whole thing was just a  scripted gag to teach kids about the  horrors of alcohol despite its important  message and clear self-awareness it was  taken off the air and rarely rebroadcast  reportedly due to backlash from parents  however it did eventually make it onto  the third volume of the tiny toons  adventures dvd collection that was  released in 2013.  nobody really has high expectations  concerning morality and ethics when it  comes to beavis and butthead the mtv  cartoon influenced an entire generation  with its crass and irreverent brand of  humor it's no surprise that a number of  episodes have been edited or even  outright banned but there's one  particularly infamous episode that was  completely removed from circulation due  to a real world tragedy it allegedly  inspired as reported by the new york  times a five-year-old boy from ohio who  saw the 1993 episode comedians set his  family's mobile home on fire a month  later leading to the death of his  younger sister the episode features the  title characters burning down a comedy  club and the boy's mother claimed that  the episode promoted the idea that  playing with fire was fun  when the local nbc station reported on  the story they asked some viewers of the  show who they thought for responsibility  i don't think it's mtv's fault at all i  think it's a parent's ball for maybe  letting kids like five years old  watching the show notably this wasn't  the first time that the show was  associated with a real world fire in  ohio as there was an incident months  earlier in which three young girls  nearly burned their house down according  to the police they admitted that a  beavis and butt head gag inspired them  to use an aerosol can and a match to set  clothing on fire  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite shows are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]  you

